[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
### Short answer  
**It’s not a bug.**  
When a style **doesn’t have a `glyphs` URL**, MapLibre GL JS falls back to its *local-font mode* (added in v5.11.0).  
In that mode every name you put in `text-font` is treated exactly like a CSS `font-family` list:

1. **If the browser already has a system font with that name →** it is used.  
2. **If you have supplied a web-font via `@font-face` (or `document.fonts.load`) →** the browser loads it and uses it.  
3. **If the name cannot be resolved →** the browser falls back to its default generic font (usually “sans-serif”), which is what you’re seeing.

So the “generic font” you see simply means the font you asked for isn’t available locally to the browser.

---

## What’s really happening under the hood

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Style parsing** | MapLibre reads the style. No `glyphs` field → it knows it must use the *local-font fallback* path. |
| **Layer evaluation** | For each symbol layer, `text-font` is read. The array is interpreted as a **CSS-style fallback list** (e.g. `["My Brand Bold", "Arial Unicode MS", "sans-serif"]`). |
| **Font resolution** | The browser’s text-layout engine (TinySDF for the glyph rasterisation) asks the **CSS font-family resolution algorithm** for the first available font that matches the name. |
| **Web-font loading** | If you have declared a `@font-face` rule whose `font-family` matches the name, the browser fetches that URL, adds it to `document.fonts`, and then TinySDF rasterises the glyphs from the loaded font. |
| **Fallback** | If no matching `@font-face` exists **and** the OS does not have a system font with that exact name, the browser falls back to the next name in the list, eventually ending up at the generic family (`sans-serif`, `serif`, etc.). |
| **Rendering** | TinySDF creates an SDF bitmap for each glyph on-the-fly, so the text appears (never invisible) even when the desired font is missing. |

Because the fallback is *graceful* (the map never errors out), you’ll see text rendered, just not in the exact typeface you expected.

---

## How to get the exact font you want

### 1. Load the font as a **web-font** (the most common solution)

```css
/* Example: load Noto Sans Regular from your own CDN */
@font-face {
  font-family: "Noto Sans Regular";   /* must match the name used in text-font */
  src: url("https://example.com/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-weight: 400;
  font-style: normal;
}
```

```js
// Optional: make sure the font is loaded before the map draws
document.fonts.load('1rem "Noto Sans Regular"').then(() => {
  map.setStyle(myStyle);   // or map.triggerRepaint()
});
```

*Key points*  

* The **`font-family` value** in `@font-face` **must be identical** (including case and spaces) to the string you put in `text-font`.  
* The URL must be reachable from the page (CORS-allowed).  
* You can load multiple weights/styles by adding more `@font-face` rules with the same family name but different `font-weight`/`font-style`.

### 2. Use the style’s **`font-faces`** property (MapLibre-GL-JS ≥ 6.7.0)

If you prefer to keep the font mapping inside the style JSON (so it works the same on Native), add a `font-faces` block:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": null,               // omitted or null – we’re in local-font mode
  "font-faces": {
    "My Brand Bold": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.otf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-FFFF"]   // optional; can limit to Latin, etc.
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "road-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["My Brand Bold", "sans-serif"]
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*How it works*  

* When the map loads, MapLibre reads `font-faces`, fetches the URL(s) via the browser, and registers them under the given family name.  
* The same fallback rules apply, but now the font is guaranteed to be available even if the user’s OS doesn’t have it.

You can also change it at runtime:

```js
map.setFontFaces({
  "My Brand Bold": [
    { url: "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.otf" }
  ]
});
```

### 3. (Optional) Serve **glyph PBFs** instead of using local fonts

If you *do* want to use the classic glyph-server approach (e.g., you need the same style to work on MapLibre Native, which still requires a `glyphs` URL), you must:

1. **Provide a `glyphs` URL** that points to a server that returns `{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`.  
2. Ensure the **folder name** (`fontstack`) matches the exact string used in `text-font`.  
3. Either copy an existing glyph set (e.g., from `openmaptiles/fonts`) or generate your own with **Font Maker** or **fontnik**.

> **Tip:** For most projects you don’t need this extra step. The local-font mode is faster (no network round-trips for each Unicode range) and works offline once the web-font is cached.

### 4. Verify that the font is actually being used

*Open the browser’s dev-tools → Elements → Computed → `font-family`* on a label element (MapLibre renders each label as a `<canvas>`; you can inspect the canvas’s `font` property via the console):

```js
// Find a label canvas (they have class "maplibregl-canvas")
const canvas = document.querySelector('canvas.maplibregl-canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
console.log(ctx.font);   // should show "My Brand Bold" if it was loaded
```

Or simply watch the network tab: you should see the `.woff2/.otf` request for your font, and **no** requests for `*.pbf` glyph files.

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Text appears, but in a different weight (e.g., Regular instead of Bold) | You only loaded one weight, or the `font-weight` in the `@font-face` doesn’t match the style’s `text-font` weight. | Add separate `@font-face` rules for each weight (`font-weight: 700` for Bold) and keep the family name identical. |
| No network request for your font file (only generic fonts) | The `font-family` name in `text-font` does **not** exactly match the `font-family` in `@font-face` (case, spaces, punctuation). | Double-check spelling and whitespace. |
| CORS error when loading the font | Font URL is on a different origin without proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header. | Serve the font with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| Labels disappear after a style reload | The style JSON overwrites `font-faces` with `null` or you re-initialize the map before the font finishes loading. | Load the font first (`document.fonts.ready`) or call `map.setFontFaces()` **after** the map is created. |
| Using a custom font on MapLibre Native and it still shows generic | Native reads `font-faces` only from the style; you omitted it or used a URL that only works in the browser. | Include `font-faces` in the style and make the URL point to a location the native app can fetch (e.g., bundled assets or a reachable HTTPS endpoint). |

---

## TL;DR checklist

1. **No `glyphs` → local-font mode** (expected behavior).  
2. Ensure the exact font name you put in `text-font` is **available to the browser**:  
   - Load it via `@font-face` **or**  
   - Declare it in the style’s `font-faces` property (GL JS ≥ 6.7.0).  
3. Verify the font loads (network tab, `ctx.font` console log).  
4. If you need the same style to work on MapLibre Native **or** you want to avoid loading web-fonts, add a proper `glyphs` URL and serve the corresponding PBFs instead.

---

### Quick code example (full working minimal)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – local font fallback</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    @font-face {
      font-family: "My Brand Bold";
      src: url("https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.woff2") format("woff2");
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const style = {
    "version": 8,
    "glyphs": null,               // omitted → local-font mode
    "sources": {
      "osm": {
        "type": "raster",
        "tiles": ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
        "tileSize": 256
      }
    },
    "layers": [
      { "id": "basemap", "type": "raster", "source": "osm" },
      {
        "id": "city-labels",
        "type": "symbol",
        "source": "osm",
        "source-layer": "place_label",   // (example, adjust to your source)
        "layout": {
          "text-field": ["get", "name"],
          "text-font": ["My Brand Bold", "sans-serif"],
          "text-size": 14
        },
        "paint": { "text-color": "#000" }
      }
    ]
  };

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: style,
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open that page, and you should see the labels rendered with **My Brand Bold** (once the font loads). If you see a generic sans-serif, open the dev-tools network tab – the font request is probably failing or the name doesn’t match.

---

### Bottom line

- **No `glyphs` → expected local-font fallback.**  
- The generic font you see simply means the browser couldn’t find the font you named.  
- Load the font (via `@font-face` or `font-faces`) and the problem disappears.  

No bug to fix; just make sure the font you request is actually available to the client. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for an Android MapLibre Native app that works completely offline and draws all label text from fonts that live on the device (no glyph-PBF server).  
It follows the current MapLibre-Native spec (Android 11.13.0 + iOS 6.18.0) and the **`font-faces`** mechanism described in the *maplibre-fonts-glyphs* skill.

---

## 1. Overview of the data flow

| What the style asks for | What Native does when it is **offline** |
|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `glyphs` URL → `/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf` | **Ignored** – if the request fails (e.g. 404 or a `file:` URL that points to an empty folder) Native falls back to the `font-faces` mapping. |
| `font-faces` → `{url, unicode-range}` | **Loaded from the device** (via `file:///android_asset/...` or any other local scheme). The font file is handed to HarfBuzz for shaping, then rendered as SDF on-the-fly. |
| `text-font` array on a layer | Resolved against the names you declared in `font-faces`. If a name is missing, the layer falls back to the default system font (which may look different on each device). |

So the only thing you *must* provide is a **`font-faces`** block that points at real `.ttf/.otf/.otc` files bundled with the APK. The `glyphs` field can stay in the style (it is required by the spec) but you point it at a **dummy local URL** that will never succeed – this forces the fallback path without breaking the style.

---

## 2. Bundle the fonts in your Android project

1. **Create an assets folder** (if you don’t already have one)  

   ```
   app/src/main/assets/
   ```

2. **Add a sub-folder for the fonts** – e.g. `fonts/`.  
   Put every font you want to use there, **named exactly as you will reference them in `text-font`** (case-sensitive).  
   Example layout:

   ```
   assets/
   └─ fonts/
      ├─ NotoSans-Regular.ttf
      ├─ NotoSans-Bold.ttf
      ├─ NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf
      ├─ NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf
      └─ NotoSansCJKsc-Regular.otf
   ```

3. **Optional – shrink the fonts**  
   If you only need a subset of Unicode, you can use a tool like *pyftsubset* to create a smaller font file and then reference that file in `unicode-range`. This reduces APK size and memory usage.

---

## 3. Write the style JSON

Below is a minimal style that works offline. You can start from any existing style (e.g. the OpenMapTiles demo) and just replace the two top-level sections.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/style-spec/",
  "version": 8,
  "name": "Offline demo",
  "glyphs": "file:///android_asset/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",   // dummy – will always 404
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]   // Latin + Latin-Extended
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Bold": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSans-Bold.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Arabic": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF", "U+0750-077F", "U+08A0-08FF"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans CJK SC": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSansCJKsc-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": [
          "U+3000-30FF", "U+3400-4DBF", "U+4E00-9FFF",
          "U+F900-FAFF", "U+FF00-FFEF"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // The rest of the style (sources, layers, etc.) can be copied from
  // any online style you like – just make sure every layer that
  // displays text uses a `text-font` list that matches the keys above.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  "sources": {
    "osm": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "file:///android_asset/tiles/tileset.json"
    }
  },

  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "road-label",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "transportation_name",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["coalesce", ["get", "name"], ""],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans Regular", "Noto Sans Bold"],
        "text-size": 12
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#222"
      }
    },

    {
      "id": "arabic-label",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place_name",
      "filter": ["==", ["get", "lang"], "ar"],
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans Arabic"],
        "text-size": 13
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#004"
      }
    },

    {
      "id": "cjk-label",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place_name",
      "filter": ["==", ["get", "lang"], "zh"],
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans CJK SC"],
        "text-size": 13
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#004"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### Key points in the JSON

| Field | Why it matters for offline rendering |
|-------|--------------------------------------|
| **`glyphs`** | Must be present (spec requirement) but can point to a non-existent local path. The request will fail instantly, triggering the `font-faces` fallback. |
| **`font-faces`** | The only source of glyphs when offline. Each key (`"Noto Sans Regular"`, …) must match a name that appears in any layer’s `text-font` array. |
| **`unicode-range`** | Optional but recommended. It tells Native which characters to pull from which file, preventing the engine from loading a huge CJK font when you only need Latin. If you omit it, the whole file is loaded the first time any character from that font is needed. |
| **`text-font`** | Use the exact same string as the key in `font-faces`. You can provide a fallback list (e.g. `["Noto Sans Regular", "Noto Sans Bold"]`). The first entry that resolves to a loaded font wins. |
| **Sources** | In an offline app you’ll also need vector tiles stored locally (e.g. MBTiles or a folder of `.pbf` tiles). The example uses a `file:///android_asset/tiles/tileset.json` reference, but any local tile source works. |

---

## 4. Load the style in Android

```kotlin
import org.maplibre.android.maps.MapView
import org.maplibre.android.maps.Style
import org.maplibre.android.maps.MapLibre

class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    private lateinit var mapView: MapView

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)

        // 1️⃣ Initialise the SDK (required once per process)
        MapLibre.getInstance(this)

        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)
        mapView = findViewById(R.id.mapView)

        mapView.getMapAsync { mapboxMap ->
            // 2️⃣ Load the offline style from assets
            mapboxMap.setStyle(
                Style.Builder()
                    .fromUri("file:///android_asset/offline-style.json")
            ) { style ->

                // 3️⃣ (Optional) If you need to change fonts at runtime,
                //    you can call setFontFaces() with the same structure
                //    as the JSON above.
                // style.setFontFaces(fontFacesMap)   // Kotlin Map<String, List<FontFace>>
            }

            // 4️⃣ Set an initial camera position, etc.
            mapboxMap.cameraPosition = CameraPosition.Builder()
                .target(LatLng(48.8566, 2.3522)) // Paris
                .zoom(12.0)
                .build()
        }
    }

    override fun onStart() {
        super.onStart()
        mapView.onStart()
    }
    override fun onStop() {
        super.onStop()
        mapView.onStop()
    }
    // … (other lifecycle callbacks)
}
```

### Why this works

* `Style.Builder().fromUri("file:///android_asset/offline-style.json")` tells Native to read the JSON from the APK assets.  
* When the style is parsed, Native sees a `glyphs` URL that points to a non-existent folder. The request fails **immediately** (no network traffic).  
* The engine then looks at `font-faces`. Each entry’s `url` is also a `file:///android_asset/...` URL, which the Android `AssetManager` can resolve. The font file is loaded into memory, handed to HarfBuzz for shaping, and rendered as SDF on the fly.  
* Because the fonts are already on the device, the map works **completely offline** – no HTTP, no CORS, no external glyph server.

---

## 5. Special considerations for particular scripts

| Script | What you need to do |
|--------|--------------------|
| **Latin / Greek / Cyrillic** | Include a regular/​bold Noto Sans (or any other Latin font). No extra handling. |
| **Arabic / Hebrew** | Add the appropriate Noto Sans script font to `font-faces`. Since MapLibre Native ≥ 11.13.0 uses HarfBuzz, shaping and right-to-left layout happen automatically – **no `setRTLTextPlugin` needed**. |
| **Devanagari, Thai, Khmer, Myanmar, etc.** | Same as Arabic – just add the script-specific Noto font and a matching `unicode-range`. Native will shape the grapheme clusters correctly. |
| **CJK (Chinese, Japanese, Korean)** | Native does **not** have the `localIdeographFontFamily` shortcut that GL JS has. You must provide a CJK font via `font-faces`. The example above uses `NotoSansCJKsc-Regular.otf`. If you want a different regional variant (TC, JP, KR) just add another entry and reference it in `text-font`. |
| **Mixed-script labels** (e.g. a place name that contains both Latin and CJK) | Declare **one** `text-font` list that contains *all* needed families, e.g. `["Noto Sans Regular", "Noto Sans CJK SC"]`. The engine will pick the first font that contains the requested code point, falling back to the next entry. The `unicode-range` entries ensure each font is only consulted for the characters it actually covers. |

---

## 6. Tips for a small APK & low memory usage

1. **Subset fonts** – Use `pyftsubset` (or the online *Google Fonts Subsetter*) to keep only the Unicode blocks you really

Below is a step-by-step recipe for adding a **hillshade** layer that draws from a **raster-DEM** source in a MapLibre GL JS map.  
You can do it either by editing the style JSON directly or by using the runtime API (`map.addSource()` / `map.addLayer()`). Both approaches are shown.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.0** (hillshade support was added in 5.0) | Older versions don’t understand the `hillshade` layer type. |
| A **raster-DEM** tile source (e.g. Mapbox Terrain-RGB, OpenDEM, your own tiles) | Hillshade works only with a source whose `type` is `"raster-dem"`. |
| CORS enabled on the DEM tiles (or proxy) | The browser must be able to fetch the tiles. |
| (Optional) `terrain` source if you also want 3-D terrain | Hillshade can be used alone, but if you plan to extrude the terrain you’ll need a `terrain` source as well. |

---

## 2. Add the raster-DEM source

### a) In the style JSON (static style)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "my-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",   // or a TileJSON URL
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 14,
      "attribution": "© My DEM provider"
    }
  },
  ...
}
```

*If you already have a `terrain` source, you can reuse the same source for hillshade.*

### b) Dynamically with the JS API

```js
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', // TileJSON or template URL
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14
});
```

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

### a) In the style JSON

```json
{
  "layers": [
    // … other layers (background, water, roads, etc.) …

    {
      "id": "hillshade",
      "type": "hillshade",
      "source": "my-dem",
      "layout": {},
      "paint": {
        // Default values – you can tweak any of these
        "hillshade-illumination-direction": 335,   // azimuth of the light source (degrees)
        "hillshade-illumination-anchor": "map",   // "map" (fixed) or "viewport" (rotates with map)
        "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.5,            // vertical exaggeration (0-1)
        "hillshade-shadow-color": "#000000",
        "hillshade-highlight-color": "#ffffff",
        "hillshade-accent-color": "#000000"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### b) Dynamically with the JS API

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',          // name of the raster-dem source added above
  layout: {},                // empty object is fine
  paint: {
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 335,
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,
    // optional colour tweaks:
    // 'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000',
    // 'hillshade-highlight-color': '#fff',
    // 'hillshade-accent-color': '#555'
  }
});
```

> **Tip:** Put the hillshade layer **near the bottom** of the layer stack (just above the background but below most vector layers) so that it shades the terrain without covering other symbols.

---

## 4. (Optional) Enable 3-D terrain

If you also want the map to render 3-D terrain (extruded buildings, fly-to-pitch, etc.), add a `terrain` property that points at the same DEM source:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

or in the style JSON:

```json
"terrain": {
  "source": "my-dem",
  "exaggeration": 1.5
}
```

The hillshade will automatically use the same DEM data, and the visual result looks like a shaded relief over the 3-D surface.

---

## 5. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base vector tiles (e.g. OpenStreetMap)
        "osm": {
          type: "vector",
          url: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/tiles.json"
        },
        // DEM source – replace with your own URL
        "my-dem": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          url: "https://example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 14
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // background
        { id: "bg", type: "background", paint: { "background-color": "#e0e0e0" } },

        // hillshade (placed early so other layers sit on top)
        {
          id: "hillshade",
          type: "hillshade",
          source: "my-dem",
          paint: {
            "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315,
            "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.6
          }
        },

        // example vector layer (roads)
        {
          id: "roads",
          type: "line",
          source: "osm",
          "source-layer": "transportation",
          paint: { "line-color": "#777", "line-width": 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 45,          // tilt to see the shading
    bearing: 0
  });

  // Optional: enable 3-D terrain
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `https://example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` with the URL of your own raster-DEM tiles.*

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Hillshade is missing or appears as a solid colour** | DEM source not reachable (404, CORS) or `type` not `"raster-dem"` | Verify the tile URL works in the browser, add proper CORS headers, and ensure `type: "raster-dem"` |
| **No shading at all (flat colour)** | `hillshade-exaggeration` set to `0` or very low | Increase the value (0.2-1.0 is typical) |
| **Shading looks wrong after map rotation** | `hillshade-illumination-anchor` left at default `"viewport"` while you expect a fixed sun direction | Set `'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map'` to keep the light direction fixed relative to the map |
| **Hillshade covers other layers** | Layer order – hillshade placed above symbols | Move the hillshade layer lower in the stack (`moveLayer('hillshade', 'some-other-layer-id')`) |
| **CORS error in console** | DEM tiles served without `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header | Add the header on the tile server or proxy through a CORS-enabled service |
| **Terrain looks flat but hillshade works** | `terrain` not set (or exaggeration = 0) | Call `map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1 })` |

---

## 7. Quick checklist

- [ ] **Source**: `type: "raster-dem"` with a reachable tile URL.  
- [ ] **Layer**: `type: "hillshade"` referencing that source.  
- [ ] **Paint**: Adjust `hillshade-illumination-direction`, `exaggeration`, colours as needed.  
- [ ] **Layer order**: Insert hillshade early (just above background).  
- [ ] **Optional terrain**: `map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem' })`.  
- [ ] **CORS**: Confirm the DEM tiles are CORS-enabled for the domain serving your map.  

That’s it! Once the source and layer are in place, MapLibre GL JS will render a shaded-relief image that updates automatically as you pan, zoom, or pitch the map. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that lets you keep your existing **Roboto Regular** glyph-PBFs for all Latin text **and** draw every Chinese character with a locally-hosted **Noto Sans SC** font file.  
It works on the current MapLibre GL JS (≥ 6.7 – the version that reads the `font-faces` property) without touching the `glyphs` URL or the rest of your style.

---

## 1. Host the Noto Sans SC font file

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| `NotoSansSC-Regular.otf` (or `.ttf`) – the *regular* weight is enough for most label styles. | This file will be used **only for the CJK Unicode ranges**. |
| A URL that can be fetched from the browser (e.g. `https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf`). | MapLibre loads the file through the normal browser fetch pipeline, so the URL must be reachable **and** serve the correct CORS headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain). |
| (Optional) a minified version of the file – the size is ~200 KB, which is fine for a single-page map. | Keeps the initial download small; the file is cached by the browser after the first use. |

> **Tip:** If you already have a CDN or static-file host (GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare Workers, etc.) just drop the file there and make sure the response includes `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.

---

## 2. Add a `font-faces` entry to the **root** of your style

The `font-faces` object maps a **`text-font` name** (the string you already use – `"Roboto Regular"`) to one or more font files.  
Because we only want the Noto Sans SC file to be used for Chinese characters, we restrict it with a **`unicode-range`** that covers all CJK blocks.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://my-glyphs.example.com/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "Roboto Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": [
          "U+3000-30FF",   // CJK Symbols & Punctuation
          "U+3400-4DBF",   // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension A
          "U+4E00-9FFF",   // CJK Unified Ideographs
          "U+F900-FAFF",   // CJK Compatibility Ideographs
          "U+20000-2A6DF", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension B
          "U+2A700-2B73F", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension C
          "U+2B740-2B81F", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension D
          "U+2B820-2CEAF", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension E
          "U+2F800-2FA1F"  // CJK Compatibility Ideographs Supplement
        ]
      }
    ]
  },

  /* … the rest of your style (sources, layers, etc.) … */
}
```

### What this does

* **Latin characters** (`U+0000-00FF`, etc.) are **not** covered by the `unicode-range`.  
  → MapLibre falls back to the normal glyph-PBF pipeline (`glyphs` URL) and draws them with the **Roboto Regular** glyphs you already have.

* **Chinese characters** fall inside one of the ranges above.  
  → The engine loads the **Noto Sans SC** OTF via the browser, shapes the text with the native text engine (HarfBuzz on Native, the browser’s layout engine on GL JS), and renders the result as an SDF texture.  
  → No extra glyph-PBF requests are made for CJK, keeping the network load low.

---

## 3. Load the `font-faces` into the map (if you edit the style at runtime)

If you are **editing the style JSON on the server** and the map loads it directly (`new maplibregl.Map({ style: 'style.json' })`), the `font-faces` block is read automatically – **no extra code is needed**.

If you build the style object in JavaScript (e.g. you fetch it, modify it, then call `map.setStyle(styleObj)`), you must tell the map about the new `font-faces` after the style is loaded:

```javascript
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://example.com/style.json', // or a JS object
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('styledata', () => {
  // `map.getStyle()` now contains the `font-faces` we added.
  // This call is a no-op if the style already had the block.
  map.setFontFaces(map.getStyle()['font-faces']);
});
```

> **Why call `setFontFaces`?**  
> The API existed before the style spec added `font-faces` to the root. Calling it guarantees that the map’s internal font-registry is refreshed, especially when you add the block **after** the initial style load.

---

## 4. Verify it works

1. Open the map in a browser.  
2. Open DevTools → **Network** tab, filter by **font** or **otf**. You should see a single request for `NotoSansSC-Regular.otf`.  
3. Zoom to an area with Chinese place names. The labels should now appear in the Noto Sans SC design (you can compare with the default sans-serif to be sure).  
4. Check a Latin-only area – the request for Roboto glyph PBFs (`.../Roboto%20Regular/0-255.pbf`, etc.) should still be happening, and the text should look unchanged.

If you still see the generic sans-serif:

* Make sure the **Unicode range** covers the characters you are testing (copy-paste a character into a Unicode inspector to see its code point).  
* Confirm the **CORS** header on the OTF response (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`).  
* Ensure you are running **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 6.7** (the `font-faces` feature was added in that release).  
* Clear the browser cache or add a cache-busting query string to the URL (`?v=1`) to force a fresh fetch.

---

## 5. (Optional) Keep `localIdeographFontFamily` at its default

`localIdeographFontFamily` only affects layers that **do not set a `text-font`** (i.e. they rely on the default `"sans-serif"` stack).  
Since your layers explicitly use `"Roboto Regular"`, that option does **not** interfere with the solution above, and you can leave it untouched:

```javascript
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  // …
  // localIdeographFontFamily: 'sans-serif'   // default – no change needed
});
```

If you ever add a layer that *does* rely on the default stack and you want it to use Noto Sans SC as well, you can either:

* Set `localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans SC", sans-serif'`, **or**
* Add a separate `font-faces` entry for the default `"sans-serif"` name (the same OTF + CJK range).

---

## 6. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Chinese labels with Noto Sans SC</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "glyphs": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
      "sources": {
        "osm": {
          "type": "raster",
          "tiles": ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          "tileSize": 256
        }
      },
      "layers": [
        {
          "id": "base",
          "type": "raster",
          "source": "osm"
        },
        {
          "id": "place-labels",
          "type": "symbol",
          "source": "osm",
          "layout": {
            "text-field": ["get", "name"],
            "text-font": ["Roboto Regular"],
            "text-size": 14
          },
          "paint": {
            "text-color": "#000"
          }
        }
      ],
      "font-faces": {
        "Roboto Regular": [
          {
            "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
            "unicode-range": [
              "U+3000-30FF",
              "U+3400-4DBF",
              "U+4E00-9FFF",
              "U+F900-FAFF",
              "U+20000-2A6DF",
              "U+2A700-2B73F",
              "U+2B740-2B81F",
              "U+2B820-2CEAF",
              "U+2F800-2FA1F"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    center: [116.4, 39.9], // Beijing
    zoom: 5
  });

  // If you ever replace the style later, re-apply the font-faces:
  map.on('styledata', () => {
    map.setFontFaces(map.getStyle()['font-faces']);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf` with the real URL where you host the file.*

